Why you should re-read Paradise Lost \par
\par
The greatest epic poem in the English language, John Milton\rquote s Paradise Lost, has divided critics \f1\endash  but its influence on English literature is second only to Shakespeare\rquote s, writes Benjamin Ramm.\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab\tab 19 April 2017 \par
\par
Milton\rquote s Paradise Lost is rarely read today. But this epic poem, 350 years old this month, remains a work of unparalleled imaginative genius that shapes English literature even now. \par
\par
In more than 10,000 lines of blank verse, it tells the story of the war for heaven and of man\rquote s expulsion from Eden. Its dozen sections are an ambitious attempt to comprehend the loss of paradise \endash  from the perspectives of the fallen angel Satan and of man, fallen from grace. Even to readers in a secular age, the poem is a powerful meditation on rebellion, longing and the desire for redemption. \par
\par
Despite being born into prosperity, Milton\rquote s worldview was forged by personal and political struggle. A committed republican, he rose to public prominence in the ferment of England\rquote s bloody civil war: two months after the execution of King Charles I in 1649, Milton became a diplomat for the new republic, with the title of Secretary for Foreign Tongues. (He wrote poetry in English, Greek, Latin and Italian, prose in Dutch, German, French and Spanish, and read Hebrew, Aramaic and Syriac). \par
\par
Milton gained a reputation in Europe for his erudition and rhetorical prowess in defence of England\rquote s radical new regime; at home he came to be regarded as a prolific advocate for the Commonwealth cause. But his deteriorating eyesight limited his diplomatic travels. By 1654, Milton was completely blind. For the final 20 years of his life, he would dictate his poetry, letters and polemical tracts to a series of amanuenses \endash  his daughters, friends and fellow poets. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Milton is shown dictating Paradise Lost , Picture \par
\par
Milton is shown dictating Paradise Lost to his daughters in this engraving after a painting by Michael Munkacsy (Credit: Alamy) \par
\par
In Paradise Lost, Milton draws on the classical Greek tradition to conjure the spirits of blind prophets. He invokes Homer, author of the first great epics in Western literature, and Tiresias, the oracle of Thebes who sees in his mind\rquote s eye what the physical eye cannot. As the philosopher Descartes wrote during Milton\rquote s lifetime, \ldblquote it is the soul which sees, and not the eye\rdblquote . William Blake, the most brilliant interpreter of Milton, later wrote of how \ldblquote the Eye of Imagination\rdblquote  saw beyond the narrow confines of \ldblquote Single vision\rdblquote , creating works that outlasted \ldblquote mortal vegetated Eyes\rdblquote . \par
\par
 \par
\par
Clever devil \par
\par
When Milton began Paradise Lost in 1658, he was in mourning. It was a year of public and private grief, marked by the deaths of his second wife, memorialised in his beautiful\f0\u8239?Sonnet 23, and of England\rquote s Lord Protector, Oliver Cromwell, which precipitated the gradual disintegration of the republic. Paradise Lost is an attempt to make sense of a fallen world: to \ldblquote justify the ways of God to men\rdblquote , and no doubt to Milton himself. \par
 \par
\par
Milton\rquote s religious lexicon \f1\endash  which sought to explain a \lquote fallen\rquote  world \endash  itself has fallen from use \par
\par
But these biographical aspects should not downplay the centrality of theology to the poem. As the critic Christopher Ricks wrote of Paradise Lost, \ldblquote Art for art\rquote s sake? Art for God\rquote s sake\rdblquote . One reason why Milton is read less now is that his religious lexicon \endash  which sought to explain a \lquote fallen\rquote  world \endash  itself has fallen from use. Milton the Puritan spent his life engaged in theological disputation on subjects as diverse as toleration, divorce and salvation. \par
\par
 \par
\par
John Martin\rquote s 1825 painting depicts Pandemonium , Picture \par
\par
John Martin\rquote s 1825 painting depicts Pandemonium, the capital of Hell in Paradise Lost (Credit: Alamy) \par
\par
The poem begins with Satan, the \ldblquote Traitor Angel\rdblquote , cast into hell after rebelling against his creator, God. Refusing to submit to what he calls \ldblquote the Tyranny of Heaven\rdblquote , Satan seeks revenge by tempting into sin God\rquote s precious creation: man. Milton gives a vivid account of \ldblquote Man\rquote s First Disobedience\rdblquote  before offering a guide to salvation. \par
 \par
\par
Better to reign in Hell, than serve in Heaven \endash  Milton \par
\par
Ricks notes that Paradise Lost is \ldblquote a fierce argument about God\rquote s justice\rdblquote  and that Milton\rquote s God has been deemed inflexible and cruel. By contrast, Satan has a dark charisma (\ldblquote he pleased the ear\rdblquote ) and a revolutionary demand for self-determination. His speech is peppered with the language of democratic governance (\ldblquote free choice\rdblquote , \ldblquote full consent\rdblquote , \ldblquote the popular vote\rdblquote ) \endash  and he famously declares, \ldblquote Better to reign in Hell, than serve in Heaven\rdblquote . Satan rejects God\rquote s \ldblquote splendid vassalage\rdblquote , seeking to live: \par
\par
Free, and to none accountable, preferring \par
Hard liberty before the easy yoke \par
Of servile Pomp. \par
\par
Nonconformist, anti-establishment writers such as Percy Shelley found a kindred spirit in this depiction of Satan (\ldblquote Milton\rquote s Devil as a moral being is\'85 far superior to his God\rdblquote , he wrote). Famously, William Blake, who contested the very idea of the Fall, remarked that \ldblquote The reason Milton wrote in fetters when he wrote of Angels & God, and at liberty when of Devils & Hell, is because he was a true Poet and of the Devil\rquote s party without knowing it\rdblquote . \par
 \par
\par
Milton was a true Poet and of the Devil\rquote s party without knowing it \endash  William Blake \par
\par
Like Cromwell, Milton believed his mission was to usher in the kingdom of God on earth. While he loathed the concept of the \lquote divine right of kings\rquote , Milton was willing to submit himself to God in the belief, in Benjamin Franklin's words, that \ldblquote Rebellion to Tyrants Is Obedience to God\rdblquote . \par
\par
William Blake's illustrations for Paradise Lost , Picture \par
\par
William Blake, who called Milton \lquote a true Poet\rquote , produced several sets of illustrations for Paradise Lost in the early 19th Century (Credit: Alamy) \par
\par
Although discussion of Paradise Lost often is dominated by political and theological arguments, the poem also contains a tender celebration of love. In Milton\rquote s version, Eve surrenders to temptation in part to be closer to Adam, \ldblquote the more to draw his love\rdblquote . She wishes for the freedom to err (\ldblquote What is faith, love, virtue unassayed?\rdblquote ). When she does succumb, Adam chooses to join her: \ldblquote to lose thee were to lose myself\rdblquote , he says: \par
\par
How can I live without you, how forgo \par
Thy sweet converse and love so dearly joined, \par
To live again in these wild woods forlorn? \par
Should God create another Eve, and I \par
Another rib afford, yet loss of thee \par
Would never from my heart. \par
\par
Canon fodder \par
\par
When Paradise Lost was published in London in 1667, Milton had fallen out of favour. Just months before the restoration of the Stuart monarchy in May 1660, he had published a pamphlet denouncing kingship. Now Milton was scorned, his writings were burned, and he was imprisoned in the Tower of London \endash  only narrowly escaping execution after the intercession of a fellow poet, Andrew Marvell. \par
\par
Yet Paradise Lost gained immediate acclaim even among royalists. The poet laureate John Dryden reworked Milton\rquote s epic, casting Cromwell \endash  a regicide with dictatorial tendencies \endash  in the role of Satan. Samuel Johnson ranked Paradise Lost among the highest \ldblquote productions of the human mind\rdblquote . \par
 \par
\par
Milton\rquote s style was suggestive and free from what he called \lquote the troublesome and modern bondage of rhyming\rquote  \par
\par
Romantic writers celebrated Milton both for his stance against censorship (\ldblquote Give me the liberty to know, to utter, and to argue freely according to conscience\rdblquote , Milton wrote in the pamphlet Areopagitica), and for his innovative poetic form, which was suggestive, allusive and free from what he called \ldblquote the troublesome and modern bondage of rhyming\rdblquote . Paradise Lost inspired Mary Shelley\rquote s Frankenstein, while Wordsworth began his famous sonnet\f0\u8239?London, 1802\u8239?with a plea: \ldblquote Milton! thou shouldst be living at this hour: England hath need of thee\rdblquote . \par
\par
But not all critics were so favourable. The 20th Century brought us the \lquote Milton Controversy\rquote , during which his legacy was fiercely contested. His detractors included poets TS Eliot and Ezra Pound (who wrote that \ldblquote Milton is the worst sort of poison\rdblquote ), while support came from both devout Christians (like CS Lewis) and atheists (including William Empson, for whom \ldblquote The reason why the poem is so good is that it makes God so bad\rdblquote ). Malcolm X read Paradise Lost in prison, sympathising with Satan, while AE Housman quipped that \ldblquote malt does more than Milton can / To reconcile God\rquote s ways to man\rdblquote . \par
 \par
\par
No one, not even Shakespeare, surpasses Milton in his command of the sound, the music, the weight and taste and texture of English words \f1\endash  Philip Pullman \par
\par
In recent years, Paradise Lost has found new admirers. Milton is \ldblquote our greatest public poet\rdblquote ,\f0\u8239?says\u8239?author Philip Pullman, whose acclaimed trilogy His Dark Materials was inspired by the poem (and takes its title from Book II, line 916). Pullman loves Milton\rquote s audacity \f1\endash  his declaration that he will create \ldblquote Things unattempted yet in Prose or Rhyme\rdblquote  \endash  and his musicality: \ldblquote No one, not even Shakespeare, surpasses Milton in his command of the sound, the music, the weight and taste and texture of English words\rdblquote . Pullman has declared: \ldblquote I am of the Devil\rquote s party and know it\rdblquote . \par
\par
 \par
\par
Philip Pullman\rquote s His Dark Materials , Picture \par
\par
Philip Pullman\rquote s His Dark Materials takes its name from Paradise Lost; the first book in the trilogy, The Golden Compass, was turned into a movie in 2007 (Credit: Alamy) \par
\par
Milton\rquote s enemies regarded his blindness as divine retribution, but his condition enhanced his acute musical sensibility. Pullman is enchanted by the poem\rquote s \ldblquote incantatory quality\rdblquote , and implores readers to experience it aurally: \ldblquote Rolling swells and peels of sound, powerful rhythms and rich harmonies\'85 that very form casts a spell\rdblquote . Paradise Lost makes an excellent audio book. \par
\par
It is said that Milton had fevered dreams during the writing of Paradise Lost and would wake with whole passages formulated in his mind. The first time I read the poem, I did so in a single sitting, overnight \endash  like Jacob wrestling with the Angel until morning. Each re-reading brings intoxication, exhilaration and exhaustion, and vindicates Milton\rquote s observation: \ldblquote The mind is its own place, and in itself / Can make a Heaven of Hell, a Hell of Heaven.\rdblquote  \par
